  "query_preview": "..\n\nModest mid/upper level westerlies reside over the northern Great \nLakes and Ontario this afternoon while lower column flow orients \nmore southwesterly. This has led to steady moisture advection and \nbroad morning shower activity, most of which has briefly tapered \noff. The main concern for this evening and overnight will be the \npositioning of an elevated warm front which will anchor additional \nshower activity, including the potential for a few thunderstorms. \nLatest NWP tendencies indicate a southward shift in this axis, \nfavoring areas south of I-96/696 by late evening. CAPE metrics are \nmeager at best with lapse rates AOB 6.0 C/km. No severe weather \nexpected with any embedded thunderstorm structures. However, \nboundary-layer condensation pressure deficits indicate a high \nlikelihood for areas of overnight fog. Potential exists for locally \ndense patches at times, therefore, a Dense Fog Advisory may be \nneeded on short notice.\n\nThe large-scale pattern remains intact to start the day Sunday as \nthe geopotential height field stays perturbed. Expect additional \nrounds of showers and perhaps a couple rumbles of thunder as the \nwarm sector aloft begins to retreat. Split flow moves in from west \nto east overhead after 18Z, leading to subsidence/drying. Meanwhile, \n1028 mb surface high pressure builds from Manitoba into central \nOntario, backing gradient flow northeasterly. This helps promote \nfurther drying from north to south, dissolving lingering midday \nshower activity confined to areas south of I-94. Some clouds may \nlinger into Sunday night, but temperatures should still hold around \n5-10 degrees below climatological averages.\n\nUnsettled conditions persist over the Ohio Valley early next week, \nalthough the expectation is that the ridge should remain strong \nenough and in proximity to Southeast Michigan to prevent much \nspillover of showers into the Lower Peninsula. Thermal troughing \nexpands further across the Midwest, marked by 850 mb temperatures in \nthe mid single digits (Celsius), which suggests daily highs closer \nto 10F below normal through Wednesday if not longer. A shortwave \nfeature aloft could close off somewhere over the northern Ohio \nValley late Tuesday into Wednesday. This would present the next \nopportunity for additional rainfall, in slightly more widespread \nfashion.",
